Remove the broken password gate; rebuild as a Go static site

The protected area did not restrict anything, for two independent reasons:

  1. Passwords were injected into client-side JavaScript at deploy time, so
     anyone could read them with View Source.
  2. The agreement files were published as ordinary static files at
     predictable URLs. /service-agreement.html loaded directly with no
     password involved; the form only pointed an iframe at that public URL.

A static host has no server-side compute and cannot enforce access control.

Removed protected.html and the three agreement documents, the injection step,
and the workflow step that echoed the injected line into the build log. Blank
templates now live on qcloud.systems. `go run . build` fails if a file named
like one of the removed agreements reappears.

Delete the PROTECTED_PASSWORD_SA / _NDA / _BTA repository secrets — they are
unused, and they were public while the gate was live.

Also replaces hand-maintained HTML with a Go generator (main.go, content.go,
templates/), matching qcloud.systems. Build-time validation covers missing
templates, missing documents, projects without URLs, and a missing CNAME,
which would silently drop the custom domain.

Fixes two latent bugs: CONTRIBUTING.html was linked from the hub but never
copied into the published output, and the beta testing agreement's password
placeholder was never substituted while its file was never published.

Repaletted to match qcloud.systems. README rewritten — the previous version
documented the password scheme as secure, including the claim that passwords
were "never visible in the public source code".

